% ============================================================================
% determiner-type — three named categories of determiner and what each
% actually does (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% A determiner's JOB depends on WHICH kind it is -- some determiners mark a
% noun as specific or nonspecific, some place a noun in space or time, and
% some split a group into its individual parts. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? determiner_type(article, $D)
%       % precedes_a_noun_and_identifies_it_as_specific_or_nonspecific
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `determiner_type(type, description)` carrying the source
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the
% engine returns the description WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero
% model calls, and abstains on a determiner-related term that is not one
% of these three. The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a description and
% recall the type it names:
%
%     ? determiner_type($T, refers_to_a_group_or_individual_parts_within_a_group)
%       % distributive_determiner
%
% The three determiner types, as a little truth table:
%
%     type                        description                                                        quote (verbatim)
%     --------------------------  ------------------------------------------------------------------  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%     article                     precedes_a_noun_and_identifies_it_as_specific_or_nonspecific          "An article is a determiner that precedes a noun or noun phrase and identifies it as either specific or nonspecific."
%     demonstrative_determiner    communicates_the_placement_of_a_noun_in_space_or_time                 "Demonstrative determiners, also known as demonstrative adjectives, communicate the placement of a noun in space or time."
%     distributive_determiner     refers_to_a_group_or_individual_parts_within_a_group                  "Distributive determiners refer to a group or individual parts within a group."
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from Grammarly's "What Are Determiners?"
% article, which introduces each of these three categories in its own
% complete sentence before moving on to worked examples. WebFetch-verified
% before writing (twice — the second pass specifically re-confirmed all
% three sentences were complete, with no truncation or additional clauses
% following them). `trust consensus` — Grammarly is a reputable secondary
% reference, the same tier the sibling `noun-type.adj`/`verb-type.adj`/
% `pronoun-type.adj`/`preposition-type.adj`/`conjunction-type.adj` (the
% other libraries in this directory) already use for their Grammarly
% citations. A determiner category that is NOT one of these three —
% "possessive_determiner", a real category the same page also names, but
% one whose own defining sentence bundles TWO separate facts (that it is
% the possessive form of a personal pronoun, AND that it can appear before
% a noun) plus a full inline list of examples (my, your, his, her, its,
% our, their, whose), rather than one clean single-fact sentence like the
% three tabled here — is deliberately NOT a row, so a recall ABSTAINS on
% it rather than inventing a description. (See ../README.md;
% feedback_nothing_human_authored, feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table determiner_type {
    columns type, description

    row (article, precedes_a_noun_and_identifies_it_as_specific_or_nonspecific)
    row (demonstrative_determiner, communicates_the_placement_of_a_noun_in_space_or_time)
    row (distributive_determiner, refers_to_a_group_or_individual_parts_within_a_group)

    source "An article is a determiner that precedes a noun or noun phrase and identifies it as either specific or nonspecific."
    locator "https://www.grammarly.com/blog/grammar/determiners/"
    trust consensus
}
